{"answer":"The critic's case conflates the *contents* of the stored witness with the *grounds* for acceptance. Clause by clause: (1) The complete set of accepted defenders for each attacker is uniquely determined by the grounded fixpoint — the critic concedes this (\"uniquely the union of all accepted defenders, which is deterministic\"), which directly refutes the SRC_008 nondeterminism charge. (2) The circularity concern mischaracterizes grounded semantics. In the grounded extension, node x is accepted only when all its attackers are refuted; if attacker y attacks defender x and x attacks y, standard grounded-semantics rules resolve this via the fixpoint's least-fixpoint construction — x is accepted only if y is not credulously accepted, which the iteration order does not change. The stored witness records the *result* of this fixpoint, not a dependency chain. (3) The 'committed roots validity' concern is misplaced: validity requires that every attacker be defeated by at least one accepted defender, which holds by construction since only accepted defenders appear in the set and the fixpoint guarantees each attacker of an accepted node is defeated. Independence of acceptance is not a stated validity requirement — the target explicitly disclaims minimality and does not require defenders to be accepted independently of the defended node. The witness certifies that x is in the grounded extension because all attackers are defeated; that certification is sound regardless of any syntactic circularity in the attack graph, because the fixpoint already resolved that circularity. (4) Redundancy is explicitly acknowledged and deemed acceptable. No clause of the critic's case establishes an actual violation: determinism is conceded as preserved, validity holds under the stated (non-minimal) definition, and the circularity is resolved by the fixpoint before the witness is materialized."}